{"id": 14, "ts": 1782173343.4244218, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I appreciate the focus on Candidate\u202fC\u2019s reliability and quick decision\u2011making, but I remain uneasy about endorsing him without any concrete evidence of stress resilience and given the clear concerns about his communication style and egocentric attitude. Those traits can jeopardize cockpit coordination on long\u2011haul flights.\nIn contrast, Candidate\u202fB offers solid evidence on several core criteria: he is described as \u201cvery conscientious,\u201d which directly supports operational reliability, and he \u201chandles stress very well,\u201d meeting our stress\u2011resilience requirement. His ability to assess weather conditions and his excellent computer skills demonstrate strong technical competence for international routes. While B does \u201cmake nasty remarks about his colleagues,\u201d this communication issue, though serious, is a single observable behavior that can be addressed through targeted crew\u2011resource\u2011training, whereas C\u2019s communication difficulty appears more systemic.\nGiven that we have explicit, safety\u2011critical strengths for B and only a single, remediable concern, I believe B is the stronger overall fit for the long\u2011distance pilot role.", "thoughts": "We need to produce a public message as Agent 2. We need to consider the current discussion: Agent 1 recommended Candidate C, with some reservations. Agent 3 agreed on strengths but cautioned about communication and lack of stress resilience evidence.\n\nNow as Agent 2, we need to make a focused argumentative contribution, referencing discussion, using our own notes. We have notes on all candidates. We need to decide our current recommendation (vote) and explain.\n\nWe should consider the panel: Candidate C is leading but has concerns. We have other candidates facts: A has anticipation, complex connections, spatial vision, leadership, but