 C? The note says \"excellent attention skills\"? In the earlier note: \"Candidate C can make correct decisions quickly.\" Also \"Candidate C is 100% reliable\" and \"creates a positive crew atmosphere\". The structured notes mention \"excellent attention skills\" for C? In the shared mental model, they listed \"excellent attention skills\" for C. That might be derived from \"excellent attention skills\" but not in original facts. However we should stick to original facts. The original facts: \"Candidate C can make correct decisions quickly.\" \"Candidate C is regarded as egocentric.\" \"Candidate C is not very willing to further his education.\" \"Candidate C can make correct decisions quickly.\" \"Candidate C is 100% reliable.\" \"Candidate C creates a positive crew atmosphere.\" There's no explicit \"excellent attention skills\". So we shouldn't claim that.\n\nNow we need to decide vote. Let's weigh criteria:\n\nReliability: C (100% reliable). B (very conscientious) suggests reliability but not explicit. D unknown. A unknown.\n\nStress resilience: B handles stress very well. D responds adequately to unexpected events (some resilience). C unknown. A unknown.\n\nTechnical competence: A (anticipate dangerous, complex connections, spatial vision). B (weather assessment, computer skills). C (understands complicated technology? Actually not in facts; but quick decisions may imply). D (solves problems extremely well).\n\nDecision quality: C (quick correct decisions). Others unknown.\n\nAttention: D (concentrate very well). B weak memory for numbers (attention issue). A unknown. C unknown.\n\nCrew cooperation: C creates positive crew atmosphere. A very good leadership qualities (maybe good). B makes nasty remarks (bad). D hot temper, know-it-all (bad).\n\nCommunication: B nasty remarks (bad). D know-it-all (bad). C difficulty communicating ideas (bad). A show-off (bad). So all have issues.\n\nResponsibility: D takes responsibility seriously. A leadership qualities maybe.